# DURABLE REPRO — C2, an OVER-REJECTION. Ratified as a defect by D10(b)
# ADDENDUM 3 (`docs/define-gorget/decisions.md`, end of the LOG); the fix is a
# WIDENING, not a narrowing.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg): `gg check` REJECTS with
#   error[E_BorrowConflict]: borrow conflict on `v`: cannot pass `v` and `!v`
#   in the same call — their places overlap
# because an enum-variant construction routes through the CALL-shaped aliasing
# check (where a bare arg is a BORROW) even though it is an aggregate-init
# BOUNDARY position (where a bare arg is CONSUMED). The struct and tuple
# spellings of the identical program already accept — see
# `sound_ctor_mover_sibling_accept.gg`.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef, measured this session): ggdef REJECTS this too, with the same
# `E_BorrowConflict`. That is a KNOWN, EXPECTED lag — ggdef has not implemented
# ADDENDUM 3's mover exemption for ANY aggregate-init spelling, so it also
# rejects the STRUCT and TUPLE forms that production already accepts (see
# `sound_ctor_mover_sibling_accept.gg`). The round that lands the widening owes
# it on the ggdef lane too (Core #9). ggdef is therefore NOT the authority for
# this row; the ratified ruling is.
#
# INTENDED: ACCEPT, run ASan-clean, and print the PRE-MOVE element value
# ("alpha-heap-forced-payload"). The addendum is explicit that one structural
# chokepoint means ONE predicate for aggregate construction — it cannot treat
# struct/tuple one way and enum another — so the enum widening falls out of the
# ratified boundary model.
#
# ⚠ This is the one row of the batch whose intended state is MORE permissive
# than today's. Wiring today's reject as "expected" would make an over-rejection
# canonical, which is why the assertion below is the accept.
enum E:
    One(String)
    Two(String, Vector[String])

void main():
    Vector[String] v = Vector[String]()
    v.push("alpha-heap-forced-payload")
    E e = E.Two(v[0], !v)
    match e:
        case One(s):
            print(s)
        case Two(s, rest):
            print(s)
